  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "In late June 2026, NN, Inc. announced a major expansion of its liquid cooling production for NVIDIA AI data center racks, adding 47 new machine centers in its Wuxi, China plant with 100% of this new capacity already pre-sold and tied to multi-year, multi-product awards.\nThis move effectively deepens NN's role in NVIDIA's Asia supply chain while repurposing legacy automotive capacity and targeting US$80–US$90 million of accretive new business in 2026.\nWe'll now examine how NN's fully pre-sold exp",
  "passage": "In late June 2026, NN, Inc. announced a major expansion of its liquid cooling production for NVIDIA AI data center racks, adding 47 new machine centers in its Wuxi, China plant with 100% of this new capacity already pre-sold and tied to multi-year, multi-product awards.\nThis move effectively deepens NN's role in NVIDIA's Asia supply chain while repurposing legacy automotive capacity and targeting US$80–US$90 million of accretive new business in 2026.\nWe'll now examine how NN's fully pre-sold expansion of NVIDIA-related liquid cooling capacity could reshape the company's existing investment narrative.\nWe've uncovered the 10 dividend fortresses yielding 5%+ that don't just survive market storms, but thrive in them.\nTo own NN, I think you have to believe its pivot from slower automotive and industrial markets toward higher value data center content can meaningfully change the story, even while the company is still unprofitable and carries meaningful debt. The fully pre sold NVIDIA liquid cooling expansion directly reinforces the near term growth catalyst of new program ramp ups, but it does not remove the execution and balance sheet risks…",
  "statement": "targeting US$80–US$90 million of accretive new business in 2026"
 },
 {
  "id": "G073",
  "article_id": "gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b",
  "title": "Microsoft vs. Nvidia: Which Is the Better AI Stock to Own for the Next 3 Years?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Microsoft ( MSFT +1.28% ) and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) are two heavyweights in the technology world and key players in artificial intelligence (AI). Microsoft seems to have its hand in every tech market segment, especially anything involving enterprise customers. Meanwhile, Nvidia has become the de facto leader in GPU chips used in AI data centers.\nBoth stocks have made investors very wealthy over the years. But which of these top AI stocks is the better buy for the next three years? This article ",
  "passage": "…vestment and enjoyed significant cloud computing growth as a result of the partnership, it has still struggled to establish itself as a key AI player. Users, especially enterprises, have opted for Anthropic's Claude among other AI models. As a result, Microsoft shifted to a multi-model strategy and has reportedly considered adding open-source models to Copilot Cowork to lower token costs.\nInvestors should applaud Microsoft's willingness to adapt. The company has deeply entrenched relationships with the enterprise world, which gives Microsoft more margin for error in fine-tuning its AI strategy than most companies. Plus, Microsoft's Azure remains a formidable growth engine, with a staggering $627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations.\nNvidia's data center business has grown so much that the stock has become a concentrated investment in this ongoing supercycle. That means Nvidia has a sky-high ceiling with Vera Rubin shipping soon, but it's riskier in that the floor is much lower if companies stop pouring all this money into AI infrastructure. Meanwhile, Microsoft remains highly diversified across software, cloud computing, and AI. That lowers the company's ceiling, but it's also the safer stock.\nMicrosoft's impressive track record typically earns it a premium valuation. The stock has traded at an average of almost 33 times its trailing-12-month earnings over the past decade, but trades at just 22 times its earnings toda…",
  "statement": "$627 billion in remaining commercial performance obligations"
 },
 {
  "id": "G068",
  "article_id": "gnews:c3e020d179ab025bf3ba56b0",
  "title": "Nvidia acts as backstop for customer GPUs in return for cut of cloud revenue",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "By Georgia Butler\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said:",
  "passage": "… said: \"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\n\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\"\nThis is not the first time Nvidia has signed deals like this, having previously done so with the likes of CoreWeave and Lambda in 2025.\nThe agreement with CoreWeave was signed in September 2025 and had an initial value of $6.3bn , while the deal with Lambda was valued at $1.5bn .\nContracts like these ensure that Nvidia maintains a diversified customer base and also expands its internal compute capacity for researchers when GPUs are left idle.\nThe newer contracts have the added benefit of generating income for Nvidia based on the neoclouds' success, effectively creating a win-win situation.",
  "statement": "The agreement with CoreWeave was signed in September 2025 and had an initial value of $6.3bn"
 },
