  "title": "Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-14",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington | Tom's Hardware\nNvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\nBy Jowi Morales\nIt's now harder to buy Nvidia AI ",
  "passage": "…uard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\nSources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China. This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe. Nvidia was not immediately available for comment on the news.\nAlthough the U.S. has banned the latest AI GPUs for export into China since 2022, various investigations showed Chinese companies could still easily get their hands on these coveted chips until recently. Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need. President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manuf…",
  "statement": "Nvidia was not immediately available for comment on the news."
 },
 {
  "id": "G397",
  "article_id": "gnews:2957e5c79a365952140c8cce",
  "title": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\nUnlike chips use",
  "passage": "…ompanies to build their own semiconductor supply chains.\nDeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng acknowledged in a 2024 interview that chip export restrictions posed a challenge for the company.\nPreviously, DeepSeek used a combination of Nvidia and Huawei chips. The base model supporting DeepSeek R1 was trained using Nvidia's H800 chips, a product specifically designed for the Chinese market before the US government banned its export in late 2023.\nIn recent months, the company has increasingly relied on Huawei's ecosystem. In April, DeepSeek launched its V4 model, which had been optimised for Huawei Ascend chips, while Huawei revealed that training for the V4-Flash model also used Ascend processors.\nThe launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.\nIf successfully developed, DeepSeek's inference chips would target the fastest-growing segment of the AI industry.\nAs the use of AI applications expands, computing demand is shifting from model training towards inference processes that require more efficient and energy-saving chips.\nHowever, developing AI chips is not an easy task. The semiconductor industry requires significant investment and years of development to produce competitive products.\nAnother challenge comes from US restrictions limiting Chinese companies' access to the most advanced chip manufacturing facilities and high-bandwidth memory (HBM), a key component in modern AI chips.\nAt the same time, DeepSeek has begun opening its…",
  "statement": "The launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies."
 },
