 {
  "id": "G270",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1b1ca014068fb532f116b3",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia’s Biggest Threat Is This: Everyone Is Desperate to Stop Paying Nvidia Prices.\nNVIDIA (NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells. NVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18…\nBy Omor Ibne Ehsan\nNVIDIA posted 85% revenue growth to $82 billion in Q1, but half its data center sales come from hyperscalers actively funding custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon",
  "passage": "… custom AI chip alternatives.\nAmazon, Google, Microsoft, and Meta are all building proprietary silicon to cut costs and gain negotiating leverage over NVIDIA.\nNVIDIA's NVLink Fusion pivot keeps its interconnect sticky even as hyperscalers shift compute to custom chips, but the strategy implicitly concedes the battle over GPUs.\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) owns the AI compute market, but every major customer is spending billions to buy less of what it sells.\nNVIDIA carries a $4.75 trillion market cap and sits between a 52-week low of $158.18 and a high of $236.26. Q1 FY27 revenue came in at $81.61 billion, up 85.2% year over year, with data center revenue of $75.25 billion.\nAbout 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers, the same companies bankrolling Amazon Trainium, Google TPU, Microsoft Maia and Meta MTIA.\nGrowth accelerates at NVIDIA’s scale. Management guided Q2 FY27 revenue to $91.0 billion with non-GAAP gross margin holding at 75%. Networking revenue grew 199% year over year to $14.8 billion, evidence the moat extends past GPUs into InfiniBand, Spectrum-X and NVLink.\nBlackwell Ultra is ramping, Rubin was announced, and Jensen Huang called the AI factory buildout “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history.” The dividend raised to $0.25 quarterly and an additional $80 billion buyback was authorized.\nAll of this says NVDA stock is set to keep delivering, as long as the broader market remain…",
  "statement": "About 50% of that data center number comes from hyperscalers"
 },
 {
  "id": "G099",
  "article_id": "gnews:82efe7d37ce9286b38e5aac5",
  "title": "Nvidia’s Silent AI Colonialism Is Trapping East Asia in a Fossil-Fueled Hell - The Diplomat – Asia-Pacific Current Affairs Magazine",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last – with Taiwan and South Korea bearing over 90 percent of the burden.\nBy Greenpeace East Asia and Alistair Alexander\nIn late May, during Nvidia’s CEO Jensen Huang’s visit to Taiwan, local activists from the environmental group Greenpeace intercepted him to deliver a five-layer model cake reading: “AI Needs Renewable Energy.” The unexpected stunt was a reference to Huang’s own recent industry remarks, whi",
  "passage": "…anufacturing to Taiwanese semiconductor maker TSMC. As every new generation of microchips uses more energy, water, and toxic chemicals than the last, and as the AI frenzy is driving unprecedented demands for more GPU semiconductors, the island has been left to deal with ballooning energy demands and emissions.\nTake Fab 25 as an example. This TSMC manufacturing center, to be built near Taichung city, will require at least 1 GW of power – roughly the same as the entire city of Taichung – and about 100,000 tonnes of water per day, roughly a fifth of the city’s daily water use. Across Taiwan, TSMC operates more than two dozen fabs, and is planning 10 more advanced fabs like Fab 25. As a result, TSMC alone could consume 24 percent of Taiwan’s electricity by 2030, up from over 12 percent last year.\nTo meet this surge, Taipower, Taiwan’s utility, plans to build 18 gas plants totaling about 18.2 GW, roughly 40 percent of Taiwan’s current generating capacity, largely to meet the demand of the semiconductor industry, led by TSMC. This will further lock Taiwan into fossil-fuel dependency, even as the island already relies on fossil fuels for more than 80 percent of its electricity.\nNear Taiwan, South Korea – another major Nvidia manufacturing hub – faces a similar challenge. Samsung and SK Hynix, the two local giants that produce most of Nvidia’s memory chips, are pursuing massive expansions to meet surging chip demand, with little attention to the resulting strain on electricity and emis…",
  "statement": "TSMC alone could consume 24 percent of Taiwan’s electricity by 2030, up from over 12 percent last year"
 },
 {
