  "id": "G034",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45",
  "title": "NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balanc",
  "passage": "…newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subs…",
  "statement": "agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars"
 },
 {
  "id": "G046",
  "article_id": "gnews:917648d49b17070ea08fcdc1",
  "title": "Nvidia recruits longtime Microsoft sales leader Nick Parker with $40M+ pay package",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Nick Parker, a 26-year Microsoft veteran who led the company’s worldwide commercial sales business, is leaving to become Nvidia’s new sales chief — a high-profile talent shift between two of the biggest players in the AI boom.\nParker will join Nvidia as executive vice president of worldwide field operations, effective Aug. 24, according to a regulatory filing . He succeeds Jay Puri, who is retiring after 21 years running Nvidia’s global sales operation and will stay on as a senior adviser.\n“Micr",
  "passage": "…en Huang — one of the most consequential commercial roles in the industry, overseeing the operation that sells Nvidia’s chips to the world’s largest companies.\nParker, 55, rose through OEM, device and partner sales roles at Microsoft before being named president of industry and partner sales in 2022. After a promotion this year, he served most recently as executive vice president and chief business officer of Microsoft Worldwide Sales & Solutions, reporting to Judson Althoff, CEO of Microsoft’s commercial business.\nPuri, 71, is credited with helping transform Nvidia from a consumer gaming brand into an AI infrastructure giant, building the enterprise sales operation Parker will now inherit.\nOn Thursday, Microsoft unveiled a $2.5 billion initiative called the Microsoft Frontier Company, which will embed AI engineers inside customers. It will be led by Rodrigo Kede Lima, a longtime Microsoft sales and enterprise leader, most recently president of Microsoft Asia.\nMediaTek powers more than two billion devices every year, from the most advanced AI infrastructure on the planet to the everyday devices that make life smarter, faster, and more connected. For everyone. MediaTek silicon is the common thread running through all of it. Yeah, MediaTek does that.",
  "statement": "On Thursday, Microsoft unveiled a $2.5 billion initiative called the Microsoft Frontier Company, which will embed AI engineers inside customers."
