  "article_opening": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of th",
  "passage": "… to finance large installations, which have traditionally been challenging to fund, especially for emerging companies lacking the balance sheet strength typically required for capital-intensive projects.\nThe initiative reflects a significant shift in the AI market, where demand is increasingly focused not only on training large models but also on running them in production for enterprises, software providers, and AI-native businesses that require constant access to computing resources for inference, fine-tuning, and related workloads.\nTwo companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs. These projects exemplify the scale of infrastructure Nvidia aims to facilitate through this financing model, which is linked to future usage rather than relying solely on conventional long-term commitments.\nNvidia describes these facilities as DSX AI factories tailored to customer demand in various regions. For users of AI infrastructure, this model promises faster access to computing capacity, eliminating delays associated with site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware installation typical of traditional buildouts.\nAccess to compute has been a persistent constraint across the AI sector, with many emerging companies struggling to secure the necessary capital to lock in …",
